[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router0’s routing control plane (FRR/RIP) is not running, leaving it without routes to any networks outside Location A. Mechanism: Hosts in VLAN 10 (PC4/PC5) and VLAN 20 (Laptop0) forward off-subnet traffic to their gateway 172.16.10.1/172.16.20.1 on router0. Router0 then performs a route lookup; because it lacks routes to external prefixes, it immediately returns ICMP Destination Net Unreachable, which users perceive as “cannot reach anything outside.” Localization: Router0, specifically the RIP daemon (ripd) and the uplink toward the WAN/core on eth2 (10.16.1.1) that should be peering with router1 (10.16.1.2). Evidence: On router0, “ripd is not running” and its FRR config is empty. Its kernel routing table has only connected subnets and a couple of static “K>*” routes (172.18.50/51 via 10.16.1.2), but no routes to networks like 192.168.200.0/24. A test from pc4 to 192.168.200.11 returns “From 172.16.10.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” and router0 itself says “Network is unreachable” to that destination. Meanwhile, router1/router2 are running RIP and have learned 172.16.10/20 via 10.16.1.1, so Locations B/C remain internally healthy. Root cause: FRR RIP is disabled/missing on router0, so it does not learn or advertise routes beyond Location A. Corrective fix: Start and configure ripd on router0 to participate on 10.16.1.0/24 (and other WAN-facing 10.x links as needed), or add a suitable default/static route (e.g., via 10.16.1.2). Verify router0’s route table then includes remote prefixes and external reachability from VLANs 10/20 is restored.
=== END ===